<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!-- Tuned for a low-RAM (about 2 GB) ONN streaming stick. The setup service
     copies this to the user profile so it self-heals across devices. It only
     takes effect after a Kodi restart, since Kodi reads advancedsettings.xml at
     startup. No secrets here.

     Texture resolution caps (imageres, fanartres):
     Kodi caps decoded texture size, expressed as pixel height at a 16:9 area.
     Kodi 21 defaults are imageres 540 (a 960x540 budget for posters, thumbs and
     landscape art) and fanartres 1080 (fanart and hero backgrounds). Memory per
     decoded texture scales with pixel area, so lowering these is the single
     biggest no-code memory lever left.
       imageres 405: about 44 percent less memory per poster than 540, and at TV
       viewing distance in a widget row it is visually near identical. This is
       the primary saver.
       fanartres 720: a safety cap only. Fanart and hero backgrounds are
       currently disabled in the skin settings, so this rarely bites; it caps the
       cost if a background is ever enabled.
     Fallback if a 2 GB stick still struggles: drop imageres to 360 (about 56
     percent less than 540). These caps apply only to art cached AFTER the change,
     so an existing device also needs a one-time texture cache clear (handled by
     the setup service) for them to take effect.

     Cache buffer is kept modest so playback does not compound GUI memory
     pressure; redraws are limited to dirty regions to ease the weak GPU. -->
<advancedsettings>
    <imageres>405</imageres>
    <fanartres>720</fanartres>
    <cache>
        <buffermode>1</buffermode>
        <memorysize>20971520</memorysize>
        <readfactor>4</readfactor>
    </cache>
    <gui>
        <algorithmdirtyregions>3</algorithmdirtyregions>
        <nofliptimeout>0</nofliptimeout>
    </gui>
</advancedsettings>
